# Overview
#
# Linguists call a negation that sits on the object of a verb
# no-negation, after its `no` (`a check that matches no check`). The
# object is the noun that the verb acts on. A negation on the verb is
# not-negation (`a check that does not match any check`). No-negation
# puts the verb first, so the reader takes `matches` as a positive claim
# until `no` turns it around. The fix moves the negation onto the verb
# and writes `any` before the object. The rule sits at Recommended,
# because a few phrases read as fixed idiom (`leaving no room`), and the
# fix does not make them clearer.
#
# Expression
#
# Harper's part-of-speech tagger marks each word with a tag for its word
# class. `main` has two branches. The first counts a word that carries
# `VERB`, the tag for a verb, and not `NOUN`, the tag for a noun, before
# `no`. The second counts a word that carries `NOUN` before `no`,
# because the tagger reads many verbs that end in `s` as nouns (`lists`,
# `names`, `records`).
#
# In the second branch, the word before the noun must not carry `DET`,
# the tag for a determiner, a word that introduces a noun, such as
# `the`, `a`, or `this`. After a determiner, the noun is a real noun,
# and `no` opens a clause that describes it (`a path no rule matches`).
# The tagger marks `that` as a determiner, so the guard lets `that`,
# `which`, and `who` through (`a call that names no branch`). Weir has
# no lookbehind, so the filter consumes the word before `names` and
# narrows the finding back to `names no`. The filter needs that word,
# so a sentence that opens with `Lists no` passes.
#
# `after` guards the word after `no`. It skips a comparison word, such
# as `more`, `longer`, or `sooner`, because the comparison states a
# bound (`stops no sooner than one timeout`). It skips `one` and
# `matter`, for `no one` and `no matter`. It also skips a word that
# carries `ADP`, `CCONJ`, or `SCONJ`. `ADP` is the tag for a
# preposition, a word that ties a noun to the rest of the sentence, such
# as `of`, `with`, or `by`. `CCONJ` is the tag for a conjunction that
# joins two equal parts, such as `and` or `or`. `SCONJ` is the tag for a
# conjunction that opens a clause, such as `because`. Before such a
# word, `no` is an answer (`says no to the prompt`).
#
# False Positives
#
# `be` and `have` stay out. `There is no cache` and `has no header`
# state that a thing is absent, and `does not have a header` is longer
# and no clearer.
#
# `mean`, `indicate`, `signal`, `imply`, `ensure`, `guarantee`,
# `assume`, `assert`, `verify`, `check`, `confirm`, and `expect` stay
# out, because the `no` after them opens a clause of its own (`zero
# means no limit`, `ensure no value has been sent`). The negation
# belongs to that clause, so moving it onto the verb changes the claim.
#
# The determiner guard reads only the word right before the noun, so an
# adjective between a determiner and the noun still matches (`gives
# many enhanced perks no entry`).
#
# Related Rules
#
# The Required ProseNoNegation reports `need` and `require` before `no`,
# because their fix never reads as idiom. This tier leaves both verbs
# out, so only the Required tier reports them.
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule hit 74 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 24 of 25 sampled
# hits negated a verb through its object (`selects no rows`, `makes no
# attempt`, `provides no mechanism`). The misfire was the idiom `leaving
# no room`.
expr be [is, are, was, were, be, been, being, am]
expr have [has, have, had, having]
expr clause [$mean, meant, $indicate, $signal, $imply, $ensure, $guarantee, $assume, $assert, $verify, $check, $confirm, $expect, case]
expr skip [@be, @have, @clause, $need, $require]
expr after <![more, less, fewer, longer, sooner, later, earlier, higher, lower, greater, larger, smaller, better, worse, one, matter], !ADP, !CCONJ, !SCONJ>
expr verb <VERB, !NOUN, !@skip>
expr nounverb <NOUN, !@skip>
expr main [(@verb no @after), <([that, which, who, !DET] @nounverb no @after), (@nounverb no @after)>]
let message "No-negation. Negate the verb, so `matches no check` becomes `does not match any check`."
let description "Flags a verb negated through its object with `no`."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "The check does not match any check."
allows "The file has no header."
allows "Zero means no limit."
allows "A path no rule matches stays untouched."
allows "The user says no to the prompt."
